<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Inverness</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Inverness is a city in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scottish-highlands">Highlands of Scotland</a>, at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-ness">River Ness</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moray-firth">Moray Firth</a>. The nearby hill fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/craig-phadrig">Craig Phadrig</a> and the area around the Ness are associated with the Pictish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bridei-son-of-maelchon">Bridei son of Maelchon</a>, whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a> visited in the 560s. Inverness became a royal burgh in the twelfth century and is now the administrative centre of the Highland council area.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/inverness.jpg" alt="Inverness" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Diliff · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Inverness_Castle,_Scotland_-_Diliff.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Inverness stands at the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-ness">River Ness</a>, which flows from Loch Ness, at the south-western extremity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moray-firth">Moray Firth</a>. The city lies at the northern end of the Great Glen, where the Caledonian Canal connects Loch Ness, Loch Oich and Loch Lochy. It is the northernmost city in the United Kingdom. Long regarded as the de facto capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scottish-highlands">Scottish Highlands</a>, it is the administrative centre of the Highland Council region and serves the wider Highlands and Islands, with around half a million people.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Human settlement in the area dates back to around 7000 BCE. Inverness was one of the chief strongholds of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a>. In 569 Saint <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a> visited it to convert the Pictish king Brude, who is supposed to have lived in the vitrified fort on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/craig-phadrig">Craig Phadrig</a> on the city's western edge. In the 11th century Macbeth held a castle in the city as Mormaer of Moray and Ross. King David I granted the first royal charter in the 12th century, around 1160.</p><p>In 1562 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-queen-of-scots">Mary, Queen of Scots</a>, was refused admittance to Inverness Castle by its governor, a follower of the Earl of Huntly, whom she afterwards had hanged. In 1715 the Jacobites occupied the fortress as a barracks, and the government built the first Fort George there in 1727. It surrendered to the Jacobites in 1746 and was blown up. Culloden Moor nearby saw the Battle of Culloden in 1746, the last pitched battle fought in the United Kingdom. The area was solidly Gaelic-speaking until the late 19th century, and the railway reached Inverness in 1855.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Inverness was granted city status in 2000 as a Millennium City. It was the county town of Inverness-shire before becoming the headquarters of the Highland Council. The old urban core west of the A9 had 48,248 people in 2022. The powerful Clan Mackintosh, Clan Fraser and Clan MacKenzie are closely linked to the surrounding country.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Inverness” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Power centred on fortified sites such as Burghead on the Moray Firth, the largest known Pictish fort, with its carvings of bulls, Dunnottar, Dundurn and Craig Phadrig near <b>Inverness</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-picts">Kingdom of the Picts · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Government troops under the Duke of Cumberland routed Charles Edward Stuart's army near <b>Inverness</b> in under an hour.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-16">16 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After three days of fighting, Crown forces holding Old Fort George, a medieval castle at <b>Inverness</b>, surrendered to the supporters of Charles Edward Stuart, who had threatened to blow up the castle by tunnelling under its walls.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-21">21 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
